      "dom_id": "question-122632",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14121490/assets/a032eb49f78ce7bd54de6969f030e4316f2bd3028280f73991af3fe45521733c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?",
      "worked_solution": "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=i+j,\\,K(x,y)=-i-2\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=5\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=x\\,j.$",
